    <div class="prose">
      <p>Good joiners are never short of work, until suddenly they are. The referrals slow down, a big job finishes, and there's a gap in the diary you didn't see coming. Word of mouth is a great thing to have, but it's a terrible thing to rely on, because you can't turn it up when you need it. Ads you can.</p>
      <p>I already run campaigns for joiners in Scotland, so this isn't theory. Here's how it works for the trade.</p>

      <h2>What the ads bring in</h2>
      <p>Joinery is broad, so the ads are built around the jobs you actually want more of:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Fitted furniture, wardrobes and storage</li>
        <li>Doors, skirting, architrave and second-fix</li>
        <li>Staircases and bannisters</li>
        <li>Decking, garden joinery and outdoor builds</li>
        <li>Repairs, alterations and general joinery</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Why real footage matters</h2>
      <p>A homeowner can't tell a good joiner from a bad one on paper. But show them a clean run of fitted units, a proper staircase or a tidy bit of second-fix on video, and the quality speaks for itself. So we use <strong>real footage of your work</strong>, filmed on site or from jobs you've already done, never stock. It builds trust before they've even messaged.</p>

      <h2>What the funnel asks</h2>
      <p>The ad clicks through to a page that sorts serious homeowners from browsers. For joinery it asks:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>What's the job, fitted furniture, doors, stairs, decking or a repair?</li>
        <li>Which town or postcode is it in?</li>
        <li>When are they looking to get it done?</li>
        <li>Name and the best number to reach them.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>That way a vague "can you do some work for me" becomes a proper lead with the detail you need to quote.</p>

      <h2>Common questions</h2>
    </div>

